:title("Parser state memoization test") ;
:description("Guards the production memo of the parser and the purity gate in front of it
(and of the -lb block list). The parser memoizes the result of a recursive production per
(production, position, whitespace rule, skipping) - but only of a PURE one: a production
whose reachable subtree contains a :script() carries parser state, so its result is not a
function of that key and must not be memoized or block-listed. A :script() that opens with
the directive \"pure\"; declares itself a function of the text and the position, and leaves
its production memoizable.

First and Second share the prefix Probe, and First always fails on the trailing '!', so the
parser applies Probe at position 0 exactly TWICE. Each application runs the scripts once,
unless a production around them is memoized:

  * NoteI is reached only through a chain of Identifiers (Chain -> Marked -> NoteI), so a
    walk that does not follow the Identifier links into the a-grammar would call Probe pure.
  * NoteW is reached only through the CodeChilds of the :whitespace() command of Spaced, so
    a walk that only looks at Childs would call Spaced pure.
  * NoteP is declared pure, so Nested, the recursive production around it, is memoized
    and NoteP runs only ONCE. (NoteP is not really pure - it counts its runs - which is
    exactly what makes the memo observable.)

The scripts count their runs on the persistent parser stack and Check asserts the counts,
so a wrongly memoized (or wrongly blocked) production shows up as a count of 1, a missing
memo as a count of 2, and the parse fails. Run with no flag, and with -lb on the copy of the
input named *-lb.txt (-lb turns the memo off, so there NoteP runs twice): both must print
the same line and exit 0.") ;


:startRule(Test) ;

// First always fails at the '!', which is what makes the parser apply Probe at
// position 0 a second time - the only way a memo hit can ever be observed. Every
// production on the way is recursive, so only the purity gate keeps it from the memo.
Test    = First | Second ;
First   = Probe "!" ;
Second  = Probe Check ;
Probe   = Chain Spaced Nested | "(" Probe ")" ;

// Path 1: the :script() is reachable only through Identifier links.
Chain   = Marked ;
Marked  = "a" NoteI | "(" Marked ")" ;

// Path 2: the :script() is reachable only through a :whitespace() argument.
Spaced  = :whitespace(WsNote) "b" | "<" Spaced ">" ;
WsNote  = NoteI2 ;

// Path 3: only a pure script, so the production is memoized.
Nested  = NoteP "c" | "[" Nested "]" ;

// The counters live on the stack that survives between the :script() calls of one
// parse run - exactly the parser state that a memoized result would skip.
NoteI = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var s = pop()
        if (s == null) s = {i: 0, w: 0, p: 0}
        s.i++
        push(s)
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;

NoteI2 = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var s = pop()
        if (s == null) s = {i: 0, w: 0, p: 0}
        s.w++
        push(s)
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;

NoteP = :script(~~ "pure";
    (function() {
        var s = pop()
        if (s == null) s = {i: 0, w: 0, p: 0}
        s.p++
        push(s)
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// The stateful scripts must have run once per Probe application, so twice; the
// pure one once - or twice in the -lb row, which turns the memo off and reads
// its own copy of the input so this script can tell.
Check = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var s = pop()
        if (s == null) s = {i: 0, w: 0, p: 0}
        push(s)
        var p = c.srcName().indexOf("-lb.txt") >= 0 ? 2 : 1
        if (s.i === 2 && s.w === 2 && s.p === p) {
            println("parser state memo test passed")
            return undefined
        }
        println("FAIL: the scripts ran i=" + s.i + " w=" + s.w + " p=" + s.p + " time(s), want i=2 w=2 p=" + p)
        return abnf.newToken("\x01stateful-production-was-memoized", 0)
    })()
~~) ;
